From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: cevr <seeve.c@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 14:47:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] A range's adjust-step sets an accessibility adjust's move
 apart from the drag's step

A seek bar wants 10 s per VoiceOver swipe and a free drag. A range had one
`step` for both: iOS's swipe moves by the written `step`, else a twentieth of
the span, and the same `step` snaps every drag (RangeIOS.swift `adjustRange`,
`RangeSpec.sanitize`).

`adjust-step` on `input type="range"` is the swipe's move alone (schema prop
`adjustStep`, 310; the schema's digest moves). A literal is a positive number;
on any other tag it is refused (`lower-attr-tag`). iOS's accessibility
increment and decrement move by it, clamped at the ends, and the drag still
snaps to `step`. The web writes it as `data-exact-adjust-step`, and the input
glue moves the focused range by it on an arrow key, the adjust a desktop
screen reader sends, `input` then `change`. VoiceOver on iOS Safari adjusts
through WebKit and sends no key, so it keeps WebKit's own move; macOS,
Android and the painted hosts ignore the prop.

A test performs one adjust with `type "<range>" adjust up|down` (the agent's
`type <id> adjust up|down`): the arrow key on the web, the slider's
`accessibilityIncrement`/`Decrement` on iOS; the other carriers refuse it.

Tests: contract `adjust_step_is_a_ranges_positive_number`,
`adjust_steps_parse_and_print`; RangeIOSTests' adjust-step swipe and agent
adjust; host/web/tests/range-adjust.test.mjs (real arrow keys in Chromium);
scripts/agent-input.test.mjs and agent-keys.test.mjs for the driver.
